.TH UQ 2
.SH NAME
uq: run \- Monte Carlo uncertainty quantification over a pde(2) problem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "uq.m";
uq := load Uq Uq->PATH;

template := "mesh 20x20 domain 1x1 bc clamp\en" +
	"equation diffuse diffusivity $D\en" +
	"solver gmres tolerance 1e-8 restart 30\en" +
	"time step 0.01";
(s, err) := uq->run(
	"sample D uniform 0.03 0.08\encount 200\enseed 1\en" +
	"init splat 10 10 3 1.0\enuntil 5.0\enobserve get 10 10",
	template);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Uq
answers a different question than
.IR verify (2)
does: not "does this converge at the right order" but "given
uncertainty in one input, what's the resulting spread in an output" -
the standard meaning of uncertainty quantification, one layer above
.IR pde (2)'s
own problem language, in the same declarative spirit: describe the
family of problems and what to look at, not a sampling loop written by
hand.
.PP
.I Template
is an ordinary
.IR pde (2)
spec, except it contains
.B $NAME
(a single token, exactly where a number would go) wherever the sampled
value belongs.  Nothing about sampling or statistics crosses into
.IR pde (2)
itself \(em
.I uq.b
only ever calls
.IR pde (2)'s
own
.BR newproblem ,
.BR run ,
.BR clear ,
.BR splat ,
and
.BR get ,
the same as any other caller would.
.PP
.I Uqspec
is one or more newline-separated lines:
.TP
.BI "sample " name " uniform " lo " " hi
.TP
.BI "sample " name " normal " mean " " stddev
The parameter to vary and its distribution.  Exactly one
.B sample
line is supported; sampling more than one parameter at once isn't
built yet.
.TP
.BI "count " n
How many draws.
.TP
.BI "seed " s
Optional; default 1.  The same seed reproduces the same draws.
.TP
.BI "init clear " value
.TP
.BI "init splat " cx " " cy " " radius " " value
Optional initial condition applied to each draw's field before
running (a fresh field from
.IR pde (2)'s
.B newproblem
starts at all zero, so a study observing anything other than the
zero state needs one of these).
.TP
.BI "until " t
Required: each draw runs to this total time via
.IR pde (2)'s
own
.BR run ,
which needs the template's own
.B "time step"
line.
.TP
.BI "observe get " x " " y
.TP
.B "observe mean"
The scalar quantity of interest read back from each draw's final
field: a single grid value, or the field's average (useful as a
conservation check \(em a no-flux boundary diffusion should show
near-zero spread here regardless of how much the sampled parameter
varies, even though a
.B get
at one point varies a great deal).
.PP
.B Run
draws
.I count
samples, substitutes each into
.I template
in place of
.BR $NAME ,
builds and runs a fresh problem for each, and summarizes the resulting
.I count
quantity-of-interest values.  A draw whose spec fails to parse or whose
solver fails to converge is skipped, not fatal to the whole study (a
single unstable-regime draw shouldn't kill an otherwise-good batch);
.B Summary.nfailed
counts how many were skipped.  If every draw fails,
.B run
returns an error rather than a bogus all-zero summary.
.PP
.B Summary
carries
.BR mean ,
.BR stddev ,
.BR min ,
and
.B max
over the successful draws, plus the raw
.B samples
array itself for a caller that wants its own histogram or percentiles
beyond these four numbers.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/uq.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/uq.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pde (2),
.IR verify (2),
.IR sh-uq (1)
